yrrell","party":"Independent","person_id":"tvfy_11012","person_slug":"tammy-tyrrell","vote":"aye"},{"name":"Tim Ayres","party":"Labor","person_id":"tvfy_10946","person_slug":"tim-ayres","vote":"aye"},{"name":"Tony Sheldon","party":"Labor","person_id":"tvfy_10944","person_slug":"tony-sheldon","vote":"aye"},{"name":"Tyron Whitten","party":"One Nation","person_id":"tvfy_11060","person_slug":"tyron-whitten","vote":"no"},{"name":"Varun Ghosh","party":"Labor","person_id":"tvfy_11017","person_slug":"varun-ghosh","vote":"aye"},{"name":"Wendy Askew","party":"Liberal","person_id":"tvfy_10914","person_slug":"wendy-askew","vote":"no"}],"name":"Business — Consideration of Legislation","noes":25,"question":"Jonathon Duniam Pursuant to contingent notice standing in the name of the Leader of the Opposition, I move: That so much of standing orders be suspended as would prevent further consideration of the bills without a limitation of time. I move this motion because tonight we are spending $213 billion of taxpayers' money, can I tell you, without a word of debate, without one minute of scrutiny and without one iota of interest from the Australian government senators in this place, with their bedfellows down the end there, the Australian Greens. This is the next chapter of their dirty, dodgy and dastardly deal, of which we've seen many iterations over time. But who cares about $213 billion of taxpayers' money? Let's burn it. Let's chuck it out the door. This is what is happening here. Scrutiny is a thing of the past under this Labor-Green alliance—the brave new world of moderate politics over here, which has absolutely trashed the convention of scrutiny in this place. The Senate is the house of review— Michaelia Cash Allegedly. Jonathon Duniam supposedly, as Senator Cash says. Occasionally, we get together, and we look at legislation. We interrogate it, we wonder what it means, and we try and understand how we're spending $213 billion of taxpayers' money. This mob over here, the Australian government, is not big on transparency. Of course, as we know, the Centre for Public Integrity said this is the most secretive government in Australia's history. This is another chapter to be written into its next report about how this government refuses to allow itself to be open to scrutiny. We don't even know what the details are. In fact, it was last week—I believe it was in answer to a question from Senator Cash, perhaps—that we heard that the details of the agreement between Labor and the Greens, which we've not seen, will not be tabled. Was it about an extension to their party room? Are they putting a patio on—perhaps a pergola or a hot tub? I don't know what the next chapter of ","result":"affirmative","slug":"division-federal-senate-10629","source_url":"https://theyvoteforyou.org.au/divisions/senate/2026-06-29/6"}
